40: through the full parameter disc, with its two double sections disjoint.
41: 
42: Choose the nodal multiplicative coordinate \(Z=(s-1)/(s+1)\). Its marked point
43: is
44: \[
45:  P_G=-\frac{1+\epsilon}{1-\epsilon},\qquad
46:  P_G(-\epsilon)=P_G(\epsilon)^{-1}.                    \tag{IF.3}
47: \]
48: The selected Tate parameter satisfies
49: \[
50:  \left.\frac{Q}{q_*}\right|_{q_*=0}
51:        =-\frac{1-\epsilon^2}{64}.                       \tag{IF.4}
52: \]
53: Indeed the cubic discriminant has \(t\)-derivative \(-4A\) at \(t_*\), its
54: elliptic discriminant is sixteen times that cubic discriminant, and \(c_4=16\)
55: there. The first term of \(j(Q)^{-1}\) is \(Q\), which gives (IF.4).
56: 
57: Retain the actual two chord factors
58: \[
59:  F_{21}=\frac{y+g+t x/(2g)}x,\qquad
60:  F_{12}=\frac{y-g-t x/(2g)}x .
61: \]
62: At the selected node they are \(A/(2\epsilon)\) and
63: \(-A/(2\epsilon)\). The transported actual Hom map is
64: \[
65:  \phi_{ab}=\frac{q_*}{g F_{ab}},\qquad
66:  \left.\frac{\phi_{21}}{q_*}\right|_{\rm node}=\frac2{A^2},
67:  \quad
68:  \left.\frac{\phi_{12}}{q_*}\right|_{\rm node}=-\frac2{A^2}.       \tag{IF.5}
69: \]
70: All three units (IF.3)--(IF.5) extend and are nonzero on a sufficiently small
71: full \(\epsilon\)-disc. Their full-disc parameter windings are zero. This is
72: an actual selected-cusp chart statement; it does not trivialize the exterior
73: suspension at the other singular fibres.
74: 
75: ## IF.2. The complete suspension keeps the scalar
76: 
77: Use the canonical meromorphic divisor sections to identify the old Cartier
78: line with the line of (IF.1). Translation by \(G\) commutes with the displayed
79: source isomorphism. The primitive chord lift \(U_a\), covering \(-G\), has
80: target multiplier \(X(p-G)^{-a}\); consequently
81: \[
82:  I_\epsilon U_{a,\rm old}I_\epsilon^{-1}
83:        =\epsilon^{2a}U_{a,\rm new},\qquad
84:  q_{\rm old}=\epsilon^{-4}q_* .                         \tag{IF.6}
85: \]
86: The actual suspension is
87: \[
88:  B_{a,b}=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q_{\rm old}U_{a,\rm old}^{\,b},
89:  \qquad ab=2 .
90: \]
91: Thus its transported map is exactly
92: \[
93:  I_\epsilon B_{a,b}I_\epsilon^{-1}
94:        =2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q_*U_{a,\rm new}^{\,b}.       \tag{IF.7}
95: \]
96: The exponent \(2ab-4\) is zero. In degree two the Cartier multiplier has the
97: same scalar factor at source and target; these factors cancel in the
98: conjugation as well. Therefore \(\lambda_{\rm new}=\lambda_{\rm old}\).
99: 
100: An earlier proposed \(\epsilon^4\) change counted only the \(U_a\) factor.
101: Equation (IF.6) locates its omitted \(q\) factor and (IF.7) corrects that
102: attempt. No scalar convention is changed.
103: 
104: ## IF.3. The finite root comparison retains its endpoint and lattice period
105: 
106: Let the old cap use \(z_{\rm old}^m=T^{-1}\), and the new cap use
107: \(z_{\rm new}^m=t^{-1}\). On the universal \(\epsilon\)-cover the literal arrow is
108: \[
109:  z_{\rm old}=\epsilon^{4/m}z_{\rm new}.                 \tag{IF.8}
110: \]
111: The admitted branches are the existing good III-star root
112: \((a,b,m)=(1,2,4p)\), and reduced III root
113: \((a,b,m)=(2,1,4p-1)\), with their actual norm sign \(n=\pm1\).
114: The comparison of minimal-root coordinates is
115: \[
116:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
117:   m&X'_{\rm new}&Y'_{\rm new}\\ \hline
118:  4p&x'_{\rm old}&y'_{\rm old}\\
119:  4p-1&\epsilon^{-2/m}x'_{\rm old}&\epsilon^{-3/m}y'_{\rm old}.
120:  \end{array}                                           \tag{IF.9}
121: \]
122: This follows directly by writing \(X'=z_{\rm new}^{2p}x\) and
123: \(Y'=z_{\rm new}^{3p}y\) and substituting (IF.8). A full positive
124: \(\epsilon\)-turn has endpoint \(D_{\rm new}^{-4}\). In the reduced case its
125: factors on \((z_{\rm new},X',Y')\) are
126: \((\zeta_m^{-4},\zeta_m^{-2},\zeta_m^{-3})\), since \(4p=m+1\).
127: In the good case the last two factors are one.
128: 
129: The reduced branch retains the actual central nonidentity division section.
130: On the transported square good-root lattice its chosen logarithm tends to
131: \((1+i)/2\), and
132: \[
133:                    4\log R-\log(4R)=2(1+i).             \tag{IF.10}
134: \]
135: The second logarithm is the small identity logarithm. Equation (IF.10)
136: remains a lattice term in the annular affine cochain. It is not replaced by
137: zero or multiplied by \(\log\epsilon\) with an assertion of central
138: holomorphic extension.
139: 
140: The actual full reference overlap is fixed by transporting the prescribed
141: old ordinary-to-cap overlap at one nonzero parameter, including its integral
142: delta term and finite line character. The K49 relative source/Hom atlas
143: comparison then continues that full cocycle on the cap annulus. Its finite
144: averaging retains the central character and its Cauchy splitting retains the
145: annular integer. These maps identify the two punctured parameter families.
146: They do not yet attach a central collision model.
147: 
148: ## IF.4. Half-loop endpoint at the selected cusp
149: 
150: The source equation is even in \(\epsilon\), while \(G\) changes sign. Its
151: actual endpoint isomorphism is
152: \[
153:                   \iota(x,y)=(x,-y).                    \tag{IF.11}
154: \]
155: For \(\Theta(QZ)=-Z^{-1}\Theta(Z)\) and
156: \(\Theta(Z^{-1})=-Z^{-1}\Theta(Z)\), write
157: \(f_P(Z)=\Theta(Z/P)/\Theta(Z)\). The exact identity
158: \[
159:  f_{P^{-a}}(Z^{-1})=P^a f_{P^a}(Z)
160: \]
161: gives the selected-cusp line map
162: \[
163:                   (Z,V)\longmapsto(Z^{-1},P^a V).       \tag{IF.12}
164: \]
165: Here \(P\) is a bounded unit and \(P(-\epsilon)=P^{-1}\). This map squares
166: to the identity. It sends \(h\) to \(h_{\rm target}^{-1}\), \(g\) to
167: \(g_{\rm target}\), and preserves the physical delta circle.
168: Its selected-cusp valuation map is \(\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)\); it sends
169: the two specified diagonal fans to one another by that actual map.
170: 
171: The cap endpoint of a half \(\epsilon\)-turn is
172: \(\iota D_{\rm new}^{-2}\). In the reduced case its literal factors from
173: (IF.9) are \(\zeta_m^{-1}\) on \(X'\) and
174: \(\exp(-3\pi i/m)\) on \(Y'\); the latter includes the \(y\)-sign of
175: \(\iota\). Thus the source inversion and cap root rotation are both retained.
176: The full \(\epsilon\)-turn closes the selected source reflection twice.
177: In the global rho marking the same arrow is
178: \(\mathcal R_\rho(Z,V)=(Q/Z,V)\). This is literally
179: \(h_{\rm target}\circ\mathcal I_\epsilon\), because the target
180: source period is \((Q,P^{-a})\). Thus the integral height
181: translation and the factor \(P^a\) in (IF.12) are carried by an
182: actual period map; neither is dropped from a six-chart closing.
183: 
184: The root coordinate used in the other infinity chart is
185: \(\zeta=\epsilon/\sqrt{1-\epsilon^2}\), with the square root equal to one
186: at zero. The ratio is a nonzero unit and has zero winding. A positive
187: \(\epsilon\)-turn covers a negative double \(v\)-turn. The original
188: \(u^3=v\) endpoint is further discrete data; it is not closed by
189: discarding this covering degree.
190: 
191: ## IF.5. The unselected collision and its actual period orders
192: 
193: At \(\epsilon=0\) the cubic discriminant is \(4t^2(1-t)\). The selected
194: simple zero remains at \(t=1\); the unselected pair meets at \(t=0\).
195: The exact other two critical values are the analytic branches
196: \[
197:  t_\pm=\frac{\epsilon}{8}
198:  \left\{\epsilon(4-3\epsilon^2)
199:           \pm\sqrt{(9\epsilon^2-8)^3}\right\}.           \tag{IF.13}
200: \]
201: Choose the square root and the labels once. They have distinct first
202: derivatives \(\pm2i\sqrt2\) and are exchanged by \(\epsilon\mapsto-\epsilon\).
203: Set \(q_A=t-t_+\), \(q_B=t-t_-\), with the choice agreeing with the marked
204: sectional small resolution. In its retained theta/Cartier gauge,
205: \[
206:  Q=q_Aq_Bu_Q,\qquad P=q_B^{-1}u_P,\qquad
207:  c_X=q_Bu_X,                                           \tag{IF.14}
208: \]
209: where the units are retained as actual functions. The source and
210: Cartier derivation is the local chord/theta calculation of K39 applied
211: through the unit Weierstrass coordinate map described below, not a
212: replacement of the degree-two line by its generic Picard class.
213: The leading coefficients in the transported marking are
214: \[
215:                u_Q(0)=1/4096,\qquad u_P(0)=64,\qquad u_X(0)=1/8 .
216: \]
217: 
218: For completeness, a literal source map to the marked local model exists.
219: Choose either unit root \(\kappa\) of
220: \[
221:  \kappa^3-(-2+3\epsilon^2)\kappa-2\epsilon(1-\epsilon^2)
222:  =(\kappa-\epsilon)(\kappa^2+\epsilon\kappa+2-2\epsilon^2).
223: \]
224: Its central value is \(i\sqrt2\) or \(-i\sqrt2\).
225: Then \(x=\kappa^2 X_0\), \(y=\kappa^3Y_0\),
226: \(t=\kappa^4T_0\), \(v_0=g/\kappa^3\) sends (IF.1) to
227: \[
228:  Y_0^2=X_0^3+(1-2v_0)X_0^2+T_0X_0+v_0^2,\qquad G_0=(0,v_0).
229: \]
230: This map also carries the marked small resolution and the canonical
231: Cartier sections. The whole-zero site is carried to the other, distant
232: simple discriminant value. It is not changed to one of the colliding sites.
233: More explicitly, the two source heights are divided by \(\kappa^4\);
234: the earlier units \(u_Q^0,u_P^0,u_X^0\) become
235: \(\kappa^{-8}u_Q^0,\kappa^4u_P^0,\kappa^{-2}u_X^0\).
236: Their previous central values \(1/256,16,-1/4\), together with
237: \(\kappa^2=-2\), give the displayed constants. This also fixes the signs
238: of the two node labels by the chosen \(\kappa\) branch.
239: 
240: The actual periods for \(\lambda=\epsilon^N\lambda_0\) are
241: \[
242:  h=(Q,P^a),\qquad
243:  g=(P^{-b},\,2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0\epsilon^N q_*c_X^{-2}).
244:                                                                \tag{IF.15}
245: \]
246: Because \(q_*(0,0)=-1\), its collision order is zero. Their divisor matrices
247: at the two strict transforms, the parameter direction, and their sum are
248: \[
249:  P_A=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
250:  P_B=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix},\quad
251:  E=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&N\end{pmatrix},\quad
252:  H_\infty=\begin{pmatrix}2&b\\-a&N-2\end{pmatrix}.
253:                                                                \tag{IF.16}
254: \]
255: In particular \(\det H_\infty=2N-2\). These are period-order identities,
256: not a count of fixed strata, links, or characteristic numbers.
257: 
258: ## IF.6. The half-loop Cartier arrow through the collision
259: 
260: The representative \(P\sim q_B^{-1}\) in (IF.14) requires a different
261: integral Tate reindexing after the two branches are exchanged. The actual
262: target representative is
263: \[
264:                        P'=(QP)^{-1}.                   \tag{IF.17}
265: \]
266: It represents \(-G\), has the required \(q_A^{-1}\) leading order, and
267: retains the integral period omitted by the bounded selected-cusp expression.
268: Applying the theta shift identity \(a\) times gives
269: \[
270:  f_{(P')^a}(Z^{-1})
271:    =c_a Z^a f_{P^a}(Z),\qquad
272:  c_a=(-1)^aQ^{-a(a-1)/2}P^{a(1-a)}.                    \tag{IF.18}
273: \]
274: Thus the actual collision line map is
275: \[
276:               \mathcal I(Z,V)=(Z^{-1},c_a Z^aV).        \tag{IF.19}
277: \]
278: It preserves delta and squares literally to the identity:
279: \(c_a(P)c_a(P')=1\).
280: Applying the same two theta identities to the divisor of \(x\) gives
281: \[
282:                    c_X'=(QP^2)c_X .                    \tag{IF.20}
283: \]
284: Consequently the complete source and suspension periods satisfy
285: \[
286:  \mathcal I h=h'^{-1}\mathcal I,\qquad
287:  \mathcal I g=h'^{-b}g'\mathcal I.                      \tag{IF.21}
288: \]
289: For the second equality, its source coordinate has factor
290: \(P^b=Q^{-b}(P')^{-b}\). Its line coordinate has factor
291: \(P^{-ab}g_V=(P')^{-ab}(QP^2)^{-2}g_V\), since \(ab=2\).
292: This proves the full line equality including the scalar, rather than only
293: the generic Picard-point equality.
294: 
295: Let \(c=a(a-1)/2\). In the ordered valuation lattice
296: \((Z,V,q_A,q_B)\), (IF.19) and the branch exchange give
297: \[
298:  S=\begin{pmatrix}
299:  -1&0&0&0\\ a&1&-c&c\\0&0&0&1\\0&0&1&0
300:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad S^2=1,\qquad S\delta=\delta.       \tag{IF.22}
301: \]
302: Its fibre block and period relabelling are
303: \[
304:  J=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\a&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
305:  R=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
306: \]
307: The exact identities
308: \[
309:  JP_A=P_BR,\quad JP_B=P_AR,\quad JE=ER,\quad
310:  JH_\infty=H_\infty R                                  \tag{IF.23}
311: \]
312: prove compatibility with every retained period order. The nonconstant
313: unit factors in \(c_a\) stay in (IF.18); (IF.22) records only their divisors.
314: Equations (IF.18)--(IF.21) are the actual morphism relating the
315: selected-cusp and collision half-loop descriptions.
316: 
317: ## IF.7. Remaining attachment and executed scope
318: 
319: The scalar origin on the ordinary source uses \(\log q_*\). Near the
320: unselected collision \(q_*\) is a nonzero unit on the full bidisc, so this
321: logarithm is single valued there. Its \(q_A\) and \(q_B\) continuations do
322: not insert the extra \(v^{-nk}\) deck found at the selected source-zero
323: collision. Its parameter endpoint is the extending unit \(q_*^{nk}\).
324: The finite cap still carries its full norm generator and root arrow
325: (IF.8)--(IF.10).
326: 
327: This removes that particular local annular-deck mismatch. It does not
328: construct a compact global bordism: the rank-one faces of (IF.16) need
329: their actual quotient atlas, the finite-cap attachment must be written,
330: and the separate selected I1 scalar trace must be joined with its physical
331: Euler line and oriented boundary. Fixed \(\lambda\) is not asserted to
332: give uniform contraction through \(\epsilon=0\).
333: 
334: The executed script
335: \(\texttt{code/part3\_fourfold/source\_infinity\_exact.py}\) checks the
336: source equations, discriminant branches, selected units, full scalar
337: cancellation, all root-coordinate exponents, and the complete half-loop
338: source/line/period identities. Its JSON retains the explicit cap rows
339: and has global-bordism and return fields false.
340: 
341: ## IF.8. Literal rank-one sector fans and protected boundary chains
342: 
343: All three lower matrices in (IF.16) have rank one. The following is an
344: actual construction of their cone collections. It is kept in the original
345: integer lattice, with the physical cocharacter \(\delta=(0,1,0,0)\).
346: For \(P_0=P_A,P_B,E\), put
347: \[
348:  R_0(n)=(P_0n,1,0),\quad R_1(n)=(H_\infty n,0,1),\qquad
349:  T_j(x,r,s)=(x+(rP_0+sH_\infty)j,r,s).                 \tag{IF.24}
350: \]
